#X obj 688 11 env.mix;
#X obj 230 93 cnv 1 535 466 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 98 Mixes two envelopes into a single one using a mix factor
between 0 and 1\. A factor of 0 outputs the first envelope unchanged
\, a factor of 1 outputs the second envelope unchanged \, and intermediate
values produce a smooth blend of the two. The two input envelopes must
have the same number of segments.;
#X text 240 199 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 224 • Morphing between two shapes — smoothly transitioning
from one envelope shape to another \, for example from a sharp percussive
attack to a slow swell \, by changing the mix factor over time.;
#X text 250 294 • Crossfading presets — smoothly moving between two
predefined envelope presets \, for example between "short" and "long"
envelopes in a synthesizer or a sampler.;
#X text 250 349 • Controlled variation — blending a base envelope with
a randomized or modified version of itself to add subtle variation
while keeping the overall shape.;
#X text 250 404 • Expressive control — linking the mix factor to a
performance parameter (for example \, key velocity or an expression
pedal) to change the character of the envelope in real time.;
#X text 250 474 • Smooth transitions in generative music — gradually
shifting from one envelope shape to another over a long time span \,
for slowly evolving textures and algorithmic compositions.;
